ABSTRACT: Large-scale cloud ecosystems create diverse manifestations of logs, warnings and unstructured threat alerts that clog the mainstream cyber threat intelligence (CTI) platforms. Currently used correlation and rule-based systems cannot convert this disjointed evidence into real-time intelligence that can be acted upon. In this paper, a large language models (LLMs) based cyber threat intelligence extraction model is presented and customized to elastic cloud environments where semantic understanding of rapidly changing workloads and distributed attack surfaces must be constantly maintained. The architecture uses the large language models to understand the raw security telemetry data, standardize multi-source threat stories and generate high-quality intelligence products in the form of indicators of compromise, attack paths, and adversary profiles. The cloud-aware orchestration layer maps the extraction processes based on the dynamic resource states and services dependencies so that contextualized intelligent output can be generated in scale without property interrupting the operational workloads. In contrast to previous CTI automation systems, the proposed architecture focuses on semantic reasoning between logs, vulnerability feeds, and incident reports to uncover obscure relationships not accessible to signaturebased systems. The resulting intelligence can be used to proactively defend and triage incidents faster and refines security policies based on adaptive models across multi-cloud and hybrid systems, which can support resilient and autonomous cloud security evolution. © 2026 IEEE.
