[R0280] year=2026 | venue=Digest of Technical Papers - IEEE International Conference on Consumer Electronics | type=Conference paper
TITLE: ALERT: A Closed-Loop SOC Agent for Web and API Security with CVE-Based Intelligence and Adaptive Anomaly Detection
KEYWORDS: Anomaly Detection; Cyber-Physical and IoT Security; Human-in-the-Loop; SOC Automation and Adaptation; Vulnerability Intelligence; Web and API Security; Application programming interfaces (API); Authentication; Automation; Computer systems programming; Decision support systems; Embedded systems; Intelligent agents; Internet of things; Network security; Programmable logic controllers; Security systems; Anomaly detection; Applications programming interfaces; Cybe-physical and internet of thing security; Cyber physicals; Human-in-the-loop; Security operation center; Security operation center automation and adaptation; Vulnerability intelligence; Web and application programming interface security; Web programming; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Web and Application Programming Interface (API) services are central to modern digital infrastructure but remain vulnerable to injection attacks, authentication bypasses, and zero-day exploits. Traditional Security Operations Center (SOC) tools rely on static, signature-based pipelines that generate high false positives, struggle with concept drift, and provide limited decision support. This paper presents ALERT (Agentic Learning for Event-driven Response and Threat Detection), a lightweight, GPU-free (CPU-only), closed-loop SOC agent with a human-in-the-loop (HITL) that integrates Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) severity prediction, log anomaly detection, and Language Model (LLM)-assisted semantics into a cost-sensitive decision framework. ALERT ingests multimodal inputs, including vulnerability feeds, runtime logs, and analyst annotations, and dynamically adjusts responses (ALLOW, MONITOR, or BLOCK) through online policy adaptation. Evaluation on a synthetic dataset of 10,000 Apache-style logs achieved a precision of 0.991, a recall of 0.983, and an F1-score of 0.987, detecting 100 percent of critical threats while keeping false positives below 0.5 percent. Designed for scalability and transparency, ALERT's modular architecture generalizes to Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) and Internet of Things (IoT) telemetry, supporting practical, adaptive, and explainable SOC automation. © 2026 IEEE.
